# The elements of a list literal must agree on one type.
#
# Every element is one of the same list, so they have one type between
# them -- the rule an `if`'s branches obey, for the same reason. This is
# what makes `case [x, ..rest]` able to say what `x` is at all.
#
# `[]` is the exception that proves it: with no element to disagree, it
# is `[!]` and fits wherever a list is wanted (see typecheck_lists.pyk).
#
# This program is expected to FAIL type checking. Its `.ast.out` holds
# the error message, not a program's output.

mixed = [1, "two", 3]
